WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
NASA will release the first images from its Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope on December 7th. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 257. Namo UK's Lunar Landing Engine, a dragon flight home from the International Space Station, chandra turning Nebulae into sound, and a cosmic deep dive on weighing nearby stars. The Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope will, according to the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, feature in a live stream from the

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.700
American Geophysical Union Meeting in San Francisco.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:38.540
Roman launched August 30th on a Falcon Heavy, and is wrapping a three-month check-out at

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.060
the Sun Earth's L2 point.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.180
It will lean on a wide field of view and infrared vision to map huge stretches of sky and to

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:52.220
study dark energy, dark matter, and exoplanets, planets that orbit stars other than the Sun.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.920
A coronagraph which blocks a star's glare will try to image planets around other stars

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.320
directly, and NASA will hold media briefings plus a fireside chat with administrator Jared

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:03.980
Isaacman.

00:01:04.986 --> 00:01:10.466
Namo UK has completed live firings, space and defense reported, of a prototype lunar

00:01:10.466 --> 00:01:12.126
landing engine called Reliance.

00:01:12.726 --> 00:01:18.006
The six kilonewton two propellant engine burns hypergolic propellants, fuels that ignite

00:01:18.006 --> 00:01:22.846
when they meet, through a pinto injector fed by electrically driven centrifugal pumps.

00:01:22.846 --> 00:01:29.366
The test demonstrated throttling from 50% of thrust to 100%, and a preliminary design

00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:31.346
review confirmed the design is mature.

00:01:32.006 --> 00:01:36.066
An engineering model build and test campaign is scheduled for 2027.

00:01:36.826 --> 00:01:42.806
Ludovic Duvet, Argonaut team leader at the European Space Agency, or ESA, called Reliance

00:01:42.806 --> 00:01:47.766
a Key Technology Step for Europe, and said it opens applications for future lunar return

00:01:47.766 --> 00:01:48.206
missions.

00:01:48.206 --> 00:01:54.794
A SpaceX Dragon will, NASA said, undocked from the International Space Station, or ISS,

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:57.474
no earlier than November 18th.

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:02.434
The spacecraft will splash down off California no earlier than November 19th.

00:02:02.854 --> 00:02:08.154
It is bringing home a three-dimensional bioprinted blood vessel, made to study vascular aging,

00:02:08.614 --> 00:02:14.454
frozen tumor organoids, tiny lab-grown tumor models for cancer research, and microbial

00:02:14.454 --> 00:02:18.234
samples meant to examine antibiotic resistant bacteria in space.

00:02:18.994 --> 00:02:23.554
A lighting system that spent three years testing astronaut sleep and well-being is coming

00:02:23.554 --> 00:02:24.114
back to.

00:02:24.774 --> 00:02:30.094
The uncrewed dragon arrived October 15th with more than 6,300 pounds of supplies.

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:35.286
Chandra X-ray Observatory has turned four star-forming regions into sound.

00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:40.986
The album pairs Chandra X-ray data with Hubble optical images and infrared views from

00:02:40.986 --> 00:02:47.866
web and spitzer, covering M82, the Orion Nebula, the Karina Nebula, and Roe Ophiuki.

00:02:48.486 --> 00:02:53.226
In the Orion Nebula sound version, more than a thousand energetic young stars come through

00:02:53.226 --> 00:02:57.866
as twinkling sounds, and the spread out X-ray glow is mapped to high tones.

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:03.686
On the Karina Cosmic Cliffs, short string sounds go to individual baby stars, and longer

00:03:03.686 --> 00:03:05.726
notes follow the spread out X-ray glow.

00:03:05.726 --> 00:03:15.032
Curiosity rover measurements spanning more than a decade suggest Fizz.org reported that small amounts of surface water exist on Mars.

00:03:15.532 --> 00:03:25.912
Daily and seasonal swings in the carbon dioxide-rich atmosphere were analyzed, and known processes such as polar freezing of carbon dioxide cannot fully account for what was seen.

00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:33.192
What remains matches water stuck to soil grains, or water sitting in extremely thin liquid-like films at the surface.

00:03:34.407 --> 00:03:40.027
Astronomers use the National Science Foundation or NSF, very long baseline array to clock

00:03:40.027 --> 00:03:45.847
the orbit, the masses, and the distance of the Red Dwarf pair DGCVN at high precision.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:51.487
They complete a lap every 4.511 years on an ellipse that can pull them in as close

00:03:51.487 --> 00:03:54.447
as 0.59 times the Earth's sun distance.

00:03:55.127 --> 00:04:00.307
The heavier star has 39% of the sun's mass, and the other has 36%.

00:04:01.127 --> 00:04:04.227
DGCVN sits 59.3 light-years from Earth.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:09.687
Each star gives off radio waves we can detect, so the array could follow the two components

00:04:09.687 --> 00:04:13.527
separately through 27 position measurements spread over 18 years.

00:04:14.548 --> 00:04:22.208
A Type-1A Supernova has been discovered in NGC 14,333, a spiral galaxy roughly 50 million

00:04:22.208 --> 00:04:22.968
light-years away.

00:04:23.568 --> 00:04:27.888
The blast gives astronomers a target close enough for detailed follow-up observations.

00:04:27.888 --> 00:04:34.288
A Type-1A Supernova happens in a two-star system when a white dwarf, a dense leftover stellar

00:04:34.288 --> 00:04:37.648
core, explodes after pulling material off a companion.

00:04:38.737 --> 00:04:44.237
Nicer, the X-ray telescope NASA flies on the ISS, has concluded science observations

00:04:44.237 --> 00:04:47.257
after six and a half years beyond its original mission.

00:04:47.917 --> 00:04:53.357
A motor inside the instrument failed in June 2025, and it can no longer point at targets

00:04:53.357 --> 00:04:55.157
outside its present orientation.

00:04:55.157 --> 00:05:00.977
Nice or measured properties of neutron stars, the collapsed cores of dead massive stars,

00:05:01.437 --> 00:05:06.777
demonstrated pulsar-based navigation, and supported studies of black holes and active galaxies.

00:05:07.437 --> 00:05:11.157
The team will now archive eight years of data and plan decommissioning.

00:05:12.042 --> 00:05:18.162
China has signaled, Universe Today reported, an intention to place a small lander on Jupiter's moon, Calisto.

00:05:18.822 --> 00:05:22.702
The mission would be only the second landing on a solar system body beyond Mars.

00:05:22.702 --> 00:05:27.782
cameras. Accounts describe the lander as carrying two instruments, and even at that size,

00:05:27.982 --> 00:05:34.455
the scientific return is expected to be substantial. Reflect orbital plans, space.com reported,

00:05:34.935 --> 00:05:39.315
to launch its first orbital mirror next year as part of a larger constellation meant to

00:05:39.315 --> 00:05:44.295
redirect sunlight after dark. The British company has already flown an orbital test of a

00:05:44.295 --> 00:05:49.095
demo version of its laser-reflecting hardware, a step aimed at addressing worries about

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:55.759
space debris. Elsewhere, former ISS commander Oleg Scruppochka spoke, Petra reported,

00:05:56.299 --> 00:06:01.319
to more than 240 students and researchers at Princess Somaya University for technology,

00:06:01.899 --> 00:06:07.759
about life and microgravity and long-duration missions. NASA's Power to Explore Student Challenge

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:13.219
invites kindergarten through 12th grade students to design a nuclear-powered lunar base and submit

00:06:13.219 --> 00:06:18.939
a 200-word essay on a personal superpower that would aid the mission, with entries due March

00:06:18.939 --> 00:06:24.979
first 2027. That covers today's Space and Science news. Share this with a fellow space

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:29.799
enthusiast if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you tomorrow.

